<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Crocus (Alemannic king)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. <a href="/years/306">306</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Crocus was an Alemannic king in Roman service in the early fourth century. According to the late Roman Epitome de Caesaribus, he accompanied the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-i">Constantius I</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and helped bring about the proclamation of Constantius's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a> as emperor at York in 306. His career shows how Germanic leaders and their followers served in the late Roman army.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They were also part of Roman military life: according to a late Roman source, the Alemannic king <b>Crocus</b> supported the proclamation of Constantine the Great at York in 306, and several Alemannic nobles rose high in imperial service.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alemanni">Alemanni · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
